  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 223 AM MDT Wed Sep 9 2026\n\nA Freeze Warning remains in effect for the western valleys, the \nUpper Green River Basin and southern Lincoln County this morning. \nTemperatures of 26 to 32 degrees are expected again in these areas, \nwith more cold prone locations possibly dropping as low as 22 \ndegrees. \n\nOtherwise, dry conditions, clear skies and seasonal temperatures \nwill be in the forecast again today. The main hazard will once again \nbe fire weather. The lightest winds of the week are expected today, \nhowever elevated to critical fire weather conditions will be in place \ndue to critically low RH values and wind gusts up to 25 mph. These \nwinds will be primarily located from southern Lincoln County to \nNatrona County through the afternoon. The Fire Weather Watch that \nwas issued for these areas has been upgraded to Red Flag Warnings as \na result. Have also extended these new warnings into Thursday. \nBurning of any kind is highly discouraged due to the very dry \nconditions, regardless of how light the winds might be. \n\nCritical fire weather conditions will become more widespread across \nthe CWA again Thursday, as winds increase. Wind gusts of 25 to 35 \nmph will return for most areas in the afternoon. Exceptions will be \nfor locations like eastern portions of the Bighorn Basin and \nnorthern portions of Johnson County. These winds will return for \nFriday afternoon as well. The main difference will be another dry \ncold front will move across northern areas east of the Divide. \nInitial indications are this front will become stationary along \nsouthern portions of the Bighorn Basin and Johnson County by midday \nFriday. Models remain split whether the front will push further \nsouthward through the overnight hours Friday and allow for the \nassociated cold pool to infiltrate into the Bighorn Basin and \nJohnson County. \n\nMoisture will begin to increase Saturday ahead of the incoming storm \nsystem, with the western mountains and central portions of the CWA \nhaving the best chances for precipitation Saturday afternoon into \nSaturday night. Snow levels will stay around 10kft through the \nnight, so hunters/backcountry enthusiasts should prepare for snow. \nThe storm itself will move over the Cowboy State during the day \nSunday, with the coldest air staying over northwestern portions. \nSnow levels will range between 7000 and 8500ft in this area and most \nof the precipitation will stay over northern portions of the state. \nFreezing temperatures will return to western portions Monday and \nTuesday mornings in the wake of this system, with readings in the \nmiddle 30s to lower 40s elsewhere. Monday will be a crisp autumn \nday, with highs in the 60s to near 70s degrees under mostly clear \nskies.",
